@using SentenceStudio.Contracts.Coach
@inject BlazorLocalizationService Localize

@*
    What the grounding layer did to the answer this message carries.

    The defect this closes is the silent repair. An answer can reach the learner with part of it
    replaced, because what the coach originally wrote was not supported by their own data. Handing
    that over unmarked is the same failure as the refusal path had before W9: the learner reads a
    sentence and has no way to know it is not the one the coach produced.

    Five rules.

    1. COUNT-FREE, RULE-FREE. One closed state in, one localized sentence out. No count of findings,
       no rule code, no span, no server prose. A learner cannot act on "three rules fired", and
       putting it here would turn a disclosure into an audit log aimed at the wrong reader.

    2. THE TWO SILENT STATES ARE SILENT. None means checked and clean; null means not checked at
       all. They are different on the wire and a future surface may care, but neither is news to a
       learner, and a notice that renders on every ordinary turn is a notice nobody reads.

    3. UNKNOWN IS DISCLOSED, NEUTRALLY. A state this build cannot name renders a note that says
       only that — never a guess at which real state was meant. Silence is the dangerous option
       here: one of the two real states means part of the answer was rewritten, so saying nothing
       would hide a rewrite behind a version gap.

    4. NEVER BESIDE A REFUSAL. A refused turn produced no answer, so there is nothing to disclose
       about. The server sends null there, and the workspace never attaches a disclosure to a
       refused turn; this component would render nothing even if both failed, because it is given
       the suppressed value.

    5. NEVER PROMISE EVIDENCE THAT IS NOT THERE. Two of the states point the learner at the
       evidence for this answer. That pointer is conditional on THIS turn having read something,
       not on the workspace holding rows from some earlier turn — the workspace list is sticky on
       purpose, and reading it here sent a learner looking for a panel that belonged to a question
       they had already been answered.

    STATUS, NOT ALERT. role="status" with aria-live="polite". The answer is still an answer; this is
    a note about it, not a failure, and interrupting the learner mid-read would misrepresent it.

    HOST-AGNOSTIC. No base URI, no navigation manager, no platform flag.
*@

@if (Key is { } key)
{
    <div class="coach-repair-disclosure ss-caption1 text-secondary-ss mb-2"
         role="status"
         aria-live="polite"
         aria-label="@Localize["Coach_Repair_RegionLabel"]"
         data-coach-repair="@Disclosure"
         data-coach-repair-evidence="@(EvidenceOnScreen ? "true" : "false")">
        @Localize[key]
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The disclosure to state. Null and None both render nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public CoachRepairDisclosure? Disclosure { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// True when the turn this disclosure describes put its evidence on screen beside this answer.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// Turn-scoped, and the caller's job to establish. The two states that mention the evidence
    /// read differently depending on it, because "have a look at the evidence" beside an answer
    /// with no evidence is an instruction the learner cannot follow, and following it as far as
    /// they can lands them on an older turn's rows — which is worse than not being told, because
    /// they would be reading the wrong answer's working.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Deliberately not defaulted to true. A caller that forgets to pass it under-promises, which
    /// is the safe direction to fail in.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public bool EvidenceOnScreen { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The sentence for this state, or null for the two that are not news.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>None</c> and null render nothing: checked-and-clean and not-checked are the ordinary
    /// cases, and a notice that fires on every turn is a notice nobody reads.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>AnswerAltered</c> has one sentence in both columns. It reports what happened to the
    /// answer and points at nothing, so there is nothing for the evidence flag to change.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Everything else — <c>Unknown</c>, and any state a newer server adds that the tolerant
    /// converter has not collapsed — renders the neutral note. It is the one branch where saying
    /// nothing is the dangerous option: one of the two real states means part of the answer was
    /// rewritten, so silence would hide a rewrite behind a version gap. The note says only that
    /// this build cannot describe what happened, and never that anything changed.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private string? Key => Disclosure switch
    {
        null => null,
        CoachRepairDisclosure.None => null,
        CoachRepairDisclosure.AnswerAltered => "Coach_Repair_AnswerAltered",
        CoachRepairDisclosure.RepairSuppressedForLanguage => EvidenceOnScreen
            ? "Coach_Repair_SuppressedForLanguage"
            : "Coach_Repair_SuppressedForLanguage_NoEvidence",
        _ => EvidenceOnScreen
            ? "Coach_Repair_Unknown"
            : "Coach_Repair_Unknown_NoEvidence"
    };
}
